1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,361
In three candles, sellers sold 1,250 contracts.

2
00:00:09,812 --> 00:00:14,959
Yet price did not go a single tick below 2650.

3
00:00:15,408 --> 00:00:18,883
So who was buying? That is what we learn today.

4
00:00:19,883 --> 00:00:24,793
Hello, this is episode sixteen of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:25,244 --> 00:00:30,729
Last time: stacked imbalance. Where one side pushed hard.

6
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:37,136
Today: absorption and exhaustion. When there is force, but price does not move.

7
00:00:38,136 --> 00:00:45,528
Absorption: lots of aggressive orders came in, but someone on the other side soaked them all up. Price stayed put.

8
00:00:45,978 --> 00:00:52,248
Exhaustion: the push fades slowly. In the end, nobody is left to buy.

9
00:00:53,248 --> 00:01:00,092
Remember: a market order fills at once. It is aggressive, and it is what the footprint counts.

10
00:01:00,542 --> 00:01:05,060
A limit order waits in the book. It is passive.

11
00:01:05,510 --> 00:01:10,579
In absorption, passive limit orders stop the aggressive market orders.

12
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:15,705
Three candles, all with a low of 2650.

13
00:01:16,155 --> 00:01:25,247
Bid volume at 2650: 420, 380, 450. Total 1,250.

14
00:01:25,696 --> 00:01:34,604
Delta: minus 310, minus 280, minus 330. Total minus 920.

15
00:01:35,604 --> 00:01:41,717
Four signs together: very high volume. Very negative delta.

16
00:01:42,166 --> 00:01:46,529
Yet price stuck at one level, with wicks below the candles.

17
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:51,655
Meaning: a big buyer there was taking it all with limit orders.

18
00:01:52,655 --> 00:01:57,905
In this example, the sellers gave up. Price climbed to 2662.

19
00:01:58,356 --> 00:02:02,797
The sellers trapped below had stops, which came in as buys on the way up.

20
00:02:03,797 --> 00:02:07,663
Now the opposite. A rally went up to 2672.

21
00:02:08,113 --> 00:02:17,829
Watch the ask volume going up: 190, 150, 35, 9, and only 4 at the very top.

22
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:22,408
Buyers ran out. Price fell back to 2660.

23
00:02:23,408 --> 00:02:28,084
Absorption: huge volume, yet price stopped.

24
00:02:28,534 --> 00:02:33,784
Exhaustion: tiny volume, so the move stopped.

25
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:39,954
Both mean one thing: the side that was pushing is no longer winning.

26
00:02:40,954 --> 00:02:45,631
The biggest mistake: calling every big selling burst absorption.

27
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:53,892
Sometimes the wall breaks. Price broke 2650 and fell to 2641. That is a breakout.

28
00:02:54,341 --> 00:03:04,138
So wait: the level holds, the next candle closes higher, delta flips. And the stop goes below the level, like 2647.

29
00:03:05,138 --> 00:03:12,373
Do not trade a lone signal. Check the level: value area low, POC, an earlier stacked zone.

30
00:03:12,823 --> 00:03:16,533
And delta divergence, from episode fourteen.

31
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:22,940
Remember: spot forex and gold do not give real bid and ask volume.

32
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:29,346
A real footprint comes from futures. For gold, COMEX gold futures.

33
00:03:30,346 --> 00:03:36,380
So, today in short. Absorption: lots of force, yet price did not move.

34
00:03:36,829 --> 00:03:42,551
Exhaustion: the push faded, the numbers at the top kept shrinking.

35
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:47,677
And always wait for confirmation, with the stop beyond the level.

36
00:03:48,677 --> 00:03:57,507
Next episode: liquidity pools and stop hunts. Where stop losses pile up, and why big players hunt them.

37
00:03:57,957 --> 00:04:02,789
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

38
00:04:03,239 --> 00:04:09,273
